## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group Drafts

#### BBR Congestion Control
- **Presentation**: [BBR at CCWG](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-ccwg-bbr-at-ccwg-00) by Ian Swett (on behalf of Neal Cardwell).
- **Draft Reference**: draft-ietf-ccwg-bbr
- **Discussion**:
  - The draft is at version -07, focusing on refinement and matching pseudocode to Linux TCP BBRv3 and Google Quiche implementations.
  - Probe RTT filtering interval has been reverted to 10 seconds (up from 5 seconds in BBRv2), as data demonstrated better performance for BDP workloads. Congestion window (CWND) in Probe RTT is set to the maximum of 4 packets and half the estimated BDP.
  - Exiting idle logic was clarified to prevent bandwidth overestimation caused by unacknowledged packets arriving after idle periods.
  - Martin Duke raised research presented in ICCRG regarding harm measurement and coexistence regimes between BBR and Reno/CUBIC. Neal Cardwell clarified that BBRv2/v3 make bounded efforts to coexist up to BDPs of ~60 packets or 2–3 second loss epochs, but larger BDPs favor BBR by design; major architectural changes for high-BDP Reno coexistence are beyond the scope of the current draft.
  - Dan Karasy emphasized the latency and bufferbloat reductions provided by BBR relative to loss-based schemes.
  - Antonio discussed startup behavior falling behind traditional slow start; Neal Cardwell noted that using an initial window of 32 closely matches CUBIC slow start.
  - Philip inquired about client-side deployment; Ian Swett explained that client background uploads (such as photo backups on Android) utilize a tweaked scavenger variant of BBRv1 to limit bufferbloat.

#### SCReAM v2
- **Presentation**: [SCReAM V2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-ccwg-scream-v2-00) by Ingemar Johansson.
- **Draft Reference**: draft-ietf-ccwg-rfc8298bis-screamv2
- **Discussion**:
  - The draft is updated to version -03 (resolving publishing issues from -02).
  - Designed for low-latency media transport, maintaining a small queue targeted for 5G cellular access networks.
  - Introduces a new latency scaling metric based on the difference between long and short moving averages of queue delay, providing better resilience against 5G scheduling jitter.
  - When L4S is enabled, it maintains approximately two CE-marked packets per RTT. The send window lower bound is set to at least 2 packets to allow operation at very low bitrates (<100 kbps) via pacing.
  - Martin Duke and Ingemar Johansson discussed the test setups in live 5G networks and Google Meet evaluations (comparing non-L4S CC variants).
  - Ingemar outlined cross-traffic detection and remediation mechanisms designed to prevent SCReAM from starving when competing with TCP flows without L4S.
  - Implementation status: Active implementations in Ericsson testbed code, Google WebRTC, and an experimental integration into the Jitsi video bridge during the Hackathon (reported by Ian Swett).

